1
00:00:03,966 --> 00:00:04,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

2
00:00:07,029 --> 00:00:18,399
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, the sutta for today, well, the two suttas, actually, it's Yūtanikā 4524 and then the other one will be Anguttara 557.

3
00:00:18,459 --> 00:00:23,123
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, this one's very short, it says,

4
00:00:25,587 --> 00:00:32,812
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha was addressing the bhikkhus and he said, bhikkhus, whether for a lay person or for one gone forth, I do not praise the wrong way.

5
00:00:33,513 --> 00:00:44,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it is a lay person or one gone forth, who is practicing wrongly because of an undertaking the wrong way of practice, he does not attain the method, the dhamma, that is wholesome.

6
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:48,003
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, he does not develop the right view, he does not become a sotāpanna.

7
00:00:49,018 --> 00:00:50,759
[SPEAKER_00]: And what because is the wrong way?

8
00:00:51,219 --> 00:00:58,022
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the wrong view, wrong intention, wrong speech, wrong action, wrong livelihood, wrong effort, wrong leg collection and wrong composure.

9
00:00:59,002 --> 00:01:00,123
[SPEAKER_00]: This is called the wrong way.

10
00:01:00,643 --> 00:01:10,787
[SPEAKER_00]: And whether it's a layperson or one gone forth who is practicing wrongly, that is basically the reason that their practice does not lead to the attainment of the method and to the dhamma that is wholesome.

11
00:01:12,141 --> 00:01:15,964
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, because whether a lay person or or a monk, I praise the right way.

12
00:01:16,204 --> 00:01:24,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's a lay person or one going forth who is practicing rightly, because they undertake the right way of practice and they attain the method, the dhamma that is wholesome.

13
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:32,816
[SPEAKER_00]: What because is the right way, it's the right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right recollection, right composure.

14
00:01:33,216 --> 00:01:35,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's called the right way.

15
00:01:35,858 --> 00:01:41,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I thought just to kind of have this, add this sutta just as a reminder.

16
00:01:43,574 --> 00:01:57,467
[SPEAKER_00]: I think even people who have been listening to our talks, even like regularly or over a longer period of time, I think even they would, some of them, might still have a certain

17
00:01:59,661 --> 00:02:08,729
[SPEAKER_00]: There are kind of tacit assumptions and notions that the practice can be done differently if you're a householder.

18
00:02:09,149 --> 00:02:18,598
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can't ordain for whatever reason, you might feel like, well, you know, I just can't help but be excused from certain requirements.

19
00:02:19,358 --> 00:02:22,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And this sutta just cuts through all of that and says it's simply not the case.

20
00:02:24,602 --> 00:02:45,271
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and I think also, like, often what happens, you know, the five precepts, the eight precepts, the celibacy or the kind of non-celibacy, the lines get blurred and then... and also thanks to the, like, contemporary Theravāda not making it any clearer and kind of confounding the matter further,

21
00:02:47,248 --> 00:02:55,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people would even, like, read this sutra, which clearly talks about the Noble Eightfold Path, that it's the same for monks and lay people, without the difference.

22
00:02:55,852 --> 00:03:03,196
[SPEAKER_00]: It constitutes the right way or the wrong way, but they would still kind of believe, well, you know, I don't have to do it to such an extent.

23
00:03:03,256 --> 00:03:14,221
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, I can get away without being celibate, or I can get away with with my speech, you know, sometimes being kind of unwholesome, because that's just the world we live in, and so on.

24
00:03:16,202 --> 00:03:31,189
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, again, nobody forces you to follow the Noble Eightfold Path, or try to, or not, but if your goal is to practice the dhamma, then there is no other way around it.

25
00:03:31,209 --> 00:03:34,611
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, when the Buddha lists the... What constitutes the Noble Eightfold Path?

26
00:03:40,057 --> 00:03:52,399
[SPEAKER_00]: the right view, which we often talk about, you know, understanding where the suffering comes from, the Four Noble Truths, there are many different ways you could sort of... many different aspects you could use to describe it, but it kind of leads to the same understanding.

27
00:03:53,978 --> 00:03:58,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right recollection, right composure.

28
00:03:58,563 --> 00:04:10,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, right intention, like, right thought, right intention, was always defined as intention of non-sensuality, intention of non-ill-will and non-cruelty.

29
00:04:12,399 --> 00:04:12,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

30
00:04:13,550 --> 00:04:17,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Consequently, that means that the wrong intention is the intention of sensuality.

31
00:04:18,211 --> 00:04:24,093
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if one is not practicing the right intention, one is away from the path.

32
00:04:25,533 --> 00:04:28,674
[SPEAKER_00]: One will not be able to attain the method, as the Sutta says.

33
00:04:29,554 --> 00:04:33,756
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's just to illustrate a point where people think like, oh, I can...

34
00:04:33,816 --> 00:04:34,996
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to be celibate.

35
00:04:36,356 --> 00:04:38,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And I can still practice the dhamma in the right direction.

36
00:04:39,869 --> 00:04:46,592
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, if you're not... like, if you cannot physically withdraw yourself from sensuality, mentally will be inconceivable.

37
00:04:46,692 --> 00:04:53,654
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be inconceivable for you to be able to mentally not have a sensual intention, if you are still physically engaging with it.

38
00:04:55,055 --> 00:04:56,996
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said many times, you know, the simile of the sticks.

39
00:04:57,676 --> 00:05:07,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if the sticks are still floating in the river, well, no amount of your knowledge of how to light the fire can make any difference, because they're simply soaking wet.

40
00:05:08,158 --> 00:05:12,421
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to take them out of the river first, but even then, you gotta wait for them to dry.

41
00:05:12,621 --> 00:05:17,244
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's this part of drying up, like, your bodily behavior that you would have stopped.

42
00:05:17,864 --> 00:05:19,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you have to dry up your mind in regard to it.

43
00:05:19,745 --> 00:05:27,751
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, like, this work that applies on the level of right thoughts, right intentions, right images, as we mentioned in our other talks on the channel.

44
00:05:28,791 --> 00:05:33,054
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole training, it's the same.

45
00:05:34,365 --> 00:05:41,216
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you wear the robes or not, if you want to practice the dhamma, you have to follow the same path.

46
00:05:43,410 --> 00:05:55,760
[SPEAKER_00]: And, usually, the difference that the suttas kind of describe between the lay people that practice the dhamma and the monks was not in how they practiced, was not in how they got to there.

47
00:05:55,800 --> 00:06:04,768
[SPEAKER_00]: As a matter of fact, there is not a single sutta that describes a lay person's method of practice independent of talking to the Buddha, basically.

48
00:06:05,388 --> 00:06:08,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, they're like, oh, the Buddha said this is how lay people should practice towards Sotāpatti.

49
00:06:09,031 --> 00:06:10,032
[SPEAKER_00]: No such thing exists.

50
00:06:11,352 --> 00:06:25,895
[SPEAKER_00]: The suttas exist like this, where there is no differentiation between the method, and then the suttas exist of after they became Sotāpanna and noble disciples, and kind of what they returned to, or what they chose to do, whether to ordain, whether to pursue it further, or they were content with it.

51
00:06:26,515 --> 00:06:28,476
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's that's the point here.

52
00:06:28,516 --> 00:06:32,797
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, like, the the the method of getting there is the same.

53
00:06:33,568 --> 00:06:54,553
[SPEAKER_00]: According to the suttas, it would make no sense to... for lay people that the method would... that the requirement for the method is lower than for the monks, because monks would have already be given up the the lay life, the attachments, the safety of family, which means surely then there would be less requirements for the monk's practice and more for lay person's practice.

54
00:06:54,613 --> 00:06:56,714
[SPEAKER_00]: But people today like to believe the opposite.

55
00:06:57,354 --> 00:06:58,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the method was the same.

56
00:06:59,014 --> 00:07:00,935
[SPEAKER_00]: What was not the same was after

57
00:07:01,929 --> 00:07:08,099
[SPEAKER_00]: You get the right view after you attain the math, after you understand the nature of suffering, after there is no second arrow hitting you.

58
00:07:10,002 --> 00:07:11,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you content with that?

59
00:07:12,257 --> 00:07:15,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you choose to stop there, and then, okay, I'll go back.

60
00:07:16,001 --> 00:07:22,088
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not have left family, but you would have certainly been internally withdrawn from it, in terms of practicing the Noble Eightfold Path.

61
00:07:22,448 --> 00:07:31,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might choose to go back, and support, and then look after people that depend on you, or for whatever reason, or maybe your health prevents you to live like a monastic life, or so on.

62
00:07:32,899 --> 00:07:35,181
[SPEAKER_00]: But you would know that that's now not the practice.

63
00:07:35,342 --> 00:07:42,508
[SPEAKER_00]: You know exactly what practice was undertaken to get you to that method, to that understanding of the dhamma that is wholesome.

64
00:07:43,149 --> 00:07:45,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And, for many people, that might feel like more than enough.

65
00:07:46,191 --> 00:07:48,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And, in a way, it kind of is, obviously.

66
00:07:48,133 --> 00:07:49,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, it's still like... it's a major leap.

67
00:07:50,755 --> 00:07:55,279
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a bigger leap from a puthujjana to a sotāpāna than from a sotāpāna to a sakadāgāmī, in a way.

68
00:07:56,080 --> 00:08:03,506
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it was understandable that even in the suttas, the Buddha had to go around kind of prodding and sort of

69
00:08:05,345 --> 00:08:11,328
[SPEAKER_00]: There is still work to be done, and you'll regret it later, because this stood now, in your life, this life in particular,

70
00:08:26,656 --> 00:08:28,077
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the most optimal.

71
00:08:28,417 --> 00:08:38,186
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, the amount of effort that you make in this life towards the dhamma practice has the highest benefit, has the highest gain.

72
00:08:39,788 --> 00:08:41,950
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was a sutta that illustrates that.

73
00:08:41,990 --> 00:08:44,933
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was about a group of devas

74
00:08:46,078 --> 00:08:53,924
[SPEAKER_00]: We're talking to the Buddhas or something and they were... they lived in such a realm where they're all so confined, so packed up.

75
00:08:54,184 --> 00:09:01,850
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, like, I don't know, thousands of them would stand on... would be like... would take the space of a needle... a needle head.

76
00:09:02,975 --> 00:09:13,062
[SPEAKER_00]: And yet, they have no... no ill will, their minds are immeasurable, and doesn't matter how squeezed they are, basically, they're internally kind of... they're unrestricted.

77
00:09:13,882 --> 00:09:19,306
[SPEAKER_00]: And then Sariputta said, oh, that's probably because, you know, they maintain, they practice that... in that realm.

78
00:09:20,326 --> 00:09:24,089
[SPEAKER_00]: And the devas corrected him and said, actually, no, please let him know that it's not.

79
00:09:24,209 --> 00:09:26,671
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the practice we made while we were humans.

80
00:09:27,471 --> 00:09:28,532
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the human realm,

81
00:09:29,599 --> 00:09:53,239
[SPEAKER_00]: The effort, the little effort that I made, resulted being reborn in these, in these, like, obviously factually better places, where the mind is simply unconstricted, because the lift, little, what might feel like little effort now, little effort in the right direction, obviously, not any effort, but little effort in the right direction, can, as the other suttas say, be like streams of merit.

82
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:58,263
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, you could spend your whole life keeping the five precepts,

83
00:09:59,839 --> 00:10:09,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Being generous, being helpful, being kind, and that's like the base sort of threshold of meritorious behavior.

84
00:10:10,466 --> 00:10:17,931
[SPEAKER_00]: So... but you still... you wouldn't know whether it will be enough or not, or for how long you will be benefiting from it.

85
00:10:18,874 --> 00:10:35,979
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, the Buddha would go and explain, says, but if one develops the right view, cultivates the right view, even for the duration of a finger snap, or the actual jhāna, withdrawal from sensuality, thoroughly, thorough drying up, even just for a finger snap, that's like the streams of immeasurable merit.

86
00:10:38,119 --> 00:10:48,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, even you failing to actually, say, get the right view, or become an arahant,

87
00:10:48,763 --> 00:11:00,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Practicing according to the right method that he describes in this sutta will be immeasurably more meritorious than any merit that you do in a mundane sense.

88
00:11:03,048 --> 00:11:14,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another kind of encouragement as to, well, even if I fail, my failure is higher than not even trying, basically, in terms of the pure merit.

89
00:11:16,525 --> 00:11:17,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's for that sutta.

90
00:11:17,806 --> 00:11:19,087
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, kind of, it connects.

91
00:11:19,147 --> 00:11:21,268
[SPEAKER_00]: There's this... the next sutta we want to do.

92
00:11:21,288 --> 00:11:22,729
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's Anguttara 557.

93
00:11:22,849 --> 00:11:24,990
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a part about the action, but we'll get to that.

94
00:11:25,630 --> 00:11:32,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, because these are the... again, these are the five themes that should often be reflected upon a woman or a man, householder or one gone forth.

95
00:11:32,955 --> 00:11:34,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, so, no difference.

96
00:11:34,936 --> 00:11:41,960
[SPEAKER_00]: No difference in what you gotta... what method you have to follow, what requirements you have to reach and what reflections you have to do.

97
00:11:43,161 --> 00:11:43,681
[SPEAKER_00]: What five?

98
00:11:45,179 --> 00:11:56,606
[SPEAKER_00]: A woman or a man, a... a householder, or one gone forth, should reflect that I'm subject to old age, I'm not exempt from old age.

99
00:11:58,267 --> 00:12:05,272
[SPEAKER_00]: A woman or a man, in a householder gone forth, should often reflect I'm subject to illness, I'm not exempt from illness.

100
00:12:06,433 --> 00:12:09,915
[SPEAKER_00]: The third one is I'm subject to death, I'm not exempt from death.

101
00:12:10,995 --> 00:12:18,962
[SPEAKER_00]: The fourth one is, I must be, and will be, inevitably, parted, separated from everyone and everything dear and agreeable to me.

102
00:12:20,663 --> 00:12:25,207
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, eh... the fifth, it's, I am the owner of my actions, heir to my actions.

103
00:12:27,069 --> 00:12:30,372
[SPEAKER_00]: I have actions as my origin, actions as my relative, actions as my resort.

104
00:12:31,353 --> 00:12:33,915
[SPEAKER_00]: I will be the heir of whatever actions, good or bad, that I do.

105
00:12:34,035 --> 00:12:35,456
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we just talked about.

106
00:12:35,536 --> 00:12:36,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even if you don't necessarily

107
00:12:38,641 --> 00:12:44,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you won't be able to recognize the implications of your actions, because you simply don't see the bigger picture.

108
00:12:44,383 --> 00:12:49,805
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you can guess, you can have an inkling, but the implications of your actions are kind of infinite in that sense.

109
00:12:49,925 --> 00:12:58,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like... but you could know whether your actions are rooted in wholesome intentions, intentions that are aligned with the Noble Eightfold Path,

110
00:12:59,728 --> 00:13:05,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the wrong intentions, intentions of sensuality, intentions of ill-will, they are not aligned with the Noble Eightfold Path.

111
00:13:06,191 --> 00:13:16,617
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need to wait to confirm the results of your actions, because obviously, you know, your life will run out and the results would have not necessarily been depleted.

112
00:13:17,357 --> 00:13:28,983
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you know that they are rooted in wholesome, if they are rooted in the right intention, you know that nothing bad can come out of them, even in 20 eons time, so to speak.

113
00:13:30,594 --> 00:13:35,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the direction is decided in the beginning, on the basis of your intention.

114
00:13:36,778 --> 00:13:40,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no intention that's rooted in unwholesome, can result in wholesome.

115
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:44,163
[SPEAKER_00]: No intention rooted in wholesome, can result in unwholesome.

116
00:13:44,623 --> 00:13:45,423
[SPEAKER_00]: And that just remains.

117
00:13:45,443 --> 00:13:52,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it's a meritorious, in a sense, aligned with the Noble Eightfold Path, like in terms of the highest merit,

118
00:13:53,568 --> 00:14:00,132
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even need to worry about the implications and stuff, because you recognize that the roots of it are rooted in wholesome.

119
00:14:00,253 --> 00:14:02,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if the whole world blames you for it.

120
00:14:02,114 --> 00:14:11,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, you want to be celibate, you want to withdraw from sensuality as a lay person, and everybody around you is attacking you for it, and saying that you're...

121
00:14:11,239 --> 00:14:16,783
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know... you're mad, you're... it's unnatural, you shouldn't be doing it, and stuff.

122
00:14:18,266 --> 00:14:29,303
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you don't, yourself, do not see a direct benefit, if you have enough faith, in terms of the, like, practice towards the Sotāpārthi outlined by the Buddha, it will be enough to keep you doing it.

123
00:14:29,884 --> 00:14:30,966
[SPEAKER_00]: And it will be meritorious.

124
00:14:32,312 --> 00:14:32,913
[SPEAKER_00]: In and of itself.

125
00:14:32,973 --> 00:14:49,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, factually, the mind, that wild animal, is not being polluted, is not being constricted more, is not being sent down the pathways that would then have this... a lot, kind of, worse repercussions and implications.

126
00:14:49,608 --> 00:14:56,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when people act out of sensuality, they act out of a constricted context, constricted perspective, reduced perspective.

127
00:14:58,381 --> 00:15:03,024
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much where all of the world's problems come from.

128
00:15:03,044 --> 00:15:16,994
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, there's that sutta, I think, was it Dighanikā, where the Buddha says it's because of the sensuality that, I don't know, husbands fight with wives, with sons, sons with mothers, sisters, kings in the end, like kings fight with kings and everybody else, basically.

129
00:15:17,294 --> 00:15:23,159
[SPEAKER_00]: People resort to sticks, weapons, killing each other, lying, all because of the sensuality, because of their desires.

130
00:15:23,619 --> 00:15:24,860
[SPEAKER_00]: A single reason for it.

131
00:15:26,832 --> 00:15:39,978
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the sutta continues, it says, for the sake of... for the sake of what benefit should a woman, a man, householder, or one gone forth reflect that I'm subject to age, I'm not exempt from old age?

132
00:15:40,038 --> 00:15:47,321
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, beings in the youth are intoxicated with the youth and when they're intoxicated with the youth, they engage in misconduct by body, speech and mind.

133
00:15:47,401 --> 00:15:48,722
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just exactly what I was saying.

134
00:15:49,162 --> 00:15:49,382
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

135
00:15:50,859 --> 00:15:53,541
[SPEAKER_00]: You already start with a reduced perspective.

136
00:15:53,561 --> 00:15:56,484
[SPEAKER_00]: You already start with a constricted context.

137
00:15:57,384 --> 00:15:58,705
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing the bigger picture.

138
00:15:58,765 --> 00:16:03,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because the bigger picture is sort of mystically hidden away from you or something.

139
00:16:04,810 --> 00:16:07,653
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you are ignoring certain aspects of it.

140
00:16:08,393 --> 00:16:11,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually the unpleasant ones, such as I'm subject to old age.

141
00:16:11,696 --> 00:16:20,403
[SPEAKER_00]: A young person does not like to think, doesn't want, doesn't see a point in thinking about them getting old and how it will be.

142
00:16:21,963 --> 00:16:23,206
[SPEAKER_00]: And why don't they see a point?

143
00:16:23,326 --> 00:16:24,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're drunk.

144
00:16:24,509 --> 00:16:25,471
[SPEAKER_00]: They're drunk with the youth.

145
00:16:27,014 --> 00:16:29,219
[SPEAKER_00]: They simply refuse to go in that direction.

146
00:16:29,279 --> 00:16:32,706
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why the Buddha needed to encourage these reflections.

147
00:16:33,715 --> 00:16:49,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by making an effort to think those things, like in that kind of personally felt sense, not just abstractly contemplating old age or something, but in that personally felt sense, the Buddha needed to encourage people to do it, because it goes against the grain of your whole being.

148
00:16:50,045 --> 00:16:52,267
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes against what you would want to do.

149
00:16:52,307 --> 00:16:57,990
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes against the alignment of your desires and so on.

150
00:16:58,971 --> 00:17:00,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry.

151
00:17:00,372 --> 00:17:00,752
[SPEAKER_00]: So it says,

152
00:17:01,937 --> 00:17:09,461
[SPEAKER_00]: But when one often reflects upon this theme, the intoxication with youth is either completely abandoned or at least diminished.

153
00:17:10,582 --> 00:17:17,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you are intoxicated because you refuse to think about the obvious nature of your life, of your experience.

154
00:17:17,985 --> 00:17:22,028
[SPEAKER_00]: You refuse to stick to those reflections.

155
00:17:22,068 --> 00:17:23,328
[SPEAKER_00]: You refuse to cultivate them.

156
00:17:23,348 --> 00:17:27,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You refuse to allow them to kind of sink in and be felt.

157
00:17:29,051 --> 00:17:29,712
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you do,

158
00:17:30,678 --> 00:17:32,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the intoxication of the youth cannot remain.

159
00:17:33,660 --> 00:17:34,460
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be undermined.

160
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:37,342
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be eroded and eventually would evaporate or at least diminish.

161
00:17:38,862 --> 00:17:41,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why the Buddha said it's for the sake of that benefit.

162
00:17:41,984 --> 00:17:51,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's for the sake of basically sobering up in regard to your life, in regard to the permanence that that implies, ownership and everything else.

163
00:17:52,835 --> 00:17:54,056
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the next one is...

164
00:17:54,096 --> 00:17:54,677
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the same.

165
00:17:54,697 --> 00:17:57,259
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when beings are healthy, they're intoxicated with their health.

166
00:17:57,279 --> 00:18:00,422
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, they refuse to think about illness.

167
00:18:10,230 --> 00:18:20,536
[SPEAKER_00]: They kind of immediately default back to that automatic conviction that, okay, now that I'm cured, I'll be healthy forever, type of thing.

168
00:18:20,596 --> 00:18:22,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you know deep down, of course, I can't.

169
00:18:22,377 --> 00:18:24,878
[SPEAKER_00]: I will get sick, but I'll deal with it.

170
00:18:24,898 --> 00:18:26,139
[SPEAKER_00]: When it comes, I'll be okay.

171
00:18:26,219 --> 00:18:31,042
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just, again, like a carelessness that results on account of being drunk with your current health.

172
00:18:31,282 --> 00:18:33,503
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you simply don't care what happens to you in that sense.

173
00:18:34,103 --> 00:18:38,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you refuse to think about the obvious possibility of illness coming at any given time.

174
00:18:40,588 --> 00:18:44,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in and of itself, that intoxication wouldn't be a problem.

175
00:18:45,171 --> 00:18:56,618
[SPEAKER_00]: However, as the Sutta says, and as anybody honest enough would see, it would be impossible to not engage in misconduct by body, speech and mind, if you're not intoxicated.

176
00:18:58,179 --> 00:19:02,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it would be also impossible to not engage in misconduct by body, speech and mind,

177
00:19:04,132 --> 00:19:10,995
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, it's not possible to think that you can be intoxicated, but not engage in misconduct by body, speech and mind.

178
00:19:11,455 --> 00:19:14,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Intoxication automatically leads to misconduct.

179
00:19:15,176 --> 00:19:23,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you want it or not, intoxication will imply it, will carry it, will basically manifest it, the misconduct.

180
00:19:24,680 --> 00:19:29,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you could be making all the effort you want to police your conduct,

181
00:19:29,838 --> 00:19:33,881
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not undermining the intoxications, you're not uprooting anything.

182
00:19:34,421 --> 00:19:40,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Your misconduct is still there, the framework for it is still there, waiting, and sooner or later things will manifest through it.

183
00:19:41,386 --> 00:19:43,768
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you're gonna do one thing or the other rooted in it.

184
00:19:44,649 --> 00:19:53,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, yes, you start with sense restraint and precepts and withdrawal, but it's these reflections that then start to address the roots of the problem.

185
00:19:54,757 --> 00:20:05,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the intoxication with health, intoxication with youth, are removed, well, the great basis for future misconduct, for the possibility of misconduct, is removed.

186
00:20:06,486 --> 00:20:07,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are actually uprooting it.

187
00:20:09,188 --> 00:20:16,333
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is the benefit that a woman or man, householder, or one go forth, should reflect, should often reflect on the illness.

188
00:20:17,886 --> 00:20:33,996
[SPEAKER_00]: The third one is same about death, but it's the same principles as one should reflect that I am subject to death, I'm not exempt from death, because during their lives, beings are intoxicated with their life, and when they're intoxicated, they engage in misconduct by body, speech, and mind.

189
00:20:35,196 --> 00:20:39,139
[SPEAKER_00]: And this one is kind of probably the most... offers the most contrast.

190
00:20:39,179 --> 00:20:45,002
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the most obvious that when you genuinely contemplate death, you immediately, like, kind of...

191
00:20:48,310 --> 00:20:53,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Undermine all of the desires that you might still have towards anything that's based on life.

192
00:20:54,014 --> 00:20:55,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Or at least threaten them.

193
00:20:55,476 --> 00:21:00,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything will feel threatened if you actually start contemplating death.

194
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:06,104
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's only because there is still intoxication and expectation and assumption of permanence in regard to life.

195
00:21:06,844 --> 00:21:11,048
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove the intoxication, thinking about death will be the most liberating thing.

196
00:21:12,549 --> 00:21:16,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it just reveals the... that which you would have already developed.

197
00:21:16,915 --> 00:21:22,018
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why a householder or a monk should often reflect on these things.

198
00:21:22,259 --> 00:21:23,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the next one is

199
00:21:37,410 --> 00:22:04,667
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to possessions, so it's like whether a householder or a monk, the benefit for often reflecting that I must be parted and separated from everyone and everything dear and agreeable to me, is just to kind of undermine that day-to-day negligence, willful ignorance of taking up things, taking up possessions, while if you were to stop

200
00:22:05,872 --> 00:22:06,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about it.

201
00:22:06,732 --> 00:22:07,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop a context.

202
00:22:07,733 --> 00:22:12,095
[SPEAKER_00]: You will recognize that even things that are yours are not yours, really.

203
00:22:13,316 --> 00:22:17,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you... the only time... say... say you borrow something from somebody.

204
00:22:17,878 --> 00:22:27,183
[SPEAKER_00]: The only time you would basically believe or start believing that you're an owner is if you forget that it was actually borrowed.

205
00:22:27,664 --> 00:22:28,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if, say, you never knew.

206
00:22:29,405 --> 00:22:30,825
[SPEAKER_00]: You thought it was genuinely yours.

207
00:22:31,446 --> 00:22:33,507
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you discover that, no, you just borrowed it.

208
00:22:34,799 --> 00:22:50,045
[SPEAKER_00]: So, at any point of your usage of the thing that you borrowed, that you know and you make the effort to not forget that it's borrowed, at no point would you then think, oh, I'm the owner, I'm the master of this, because at any time the owner can come and ask for it.

209
00:22:50,526 --> 00:22:51,606
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're still using it.

210
00:22:53,027 --> 00:22:56,168
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to destroy it or throw it away to prove that it's not yours.

211
00:22:56,228 --> 00:23:00,330
[SPEAKER_00]: You already fully understood that it's not yours in in its own nature.

212
00:23:01,170 --> 00:23:03,031
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's kind of the nature and that's something like when people

213
00:23:04,138 --> 00:23:13,109
[SPEAKER_00]: I find difficult to understand, like, oh, how is the arahant... how does he operate on a day-to-day basis when, you know, when anatta is fully developed, nothing is his.

214
00:23:13,450 --> 00:23:13,890
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how.

215
00:23:14,803 --> 00:23:30,496
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he still has perceptions of things and significance of the things and what they can... what those things can be used for, but at no point does he fall back into that view, which he's now incapable of, that he is the owner and those things are, like, solely dependent for him.

216
00:23:30,916 --> 00:23:31,817
[SPEAKER_00]: And...

217
00:23:32,657 --> 00:23:39,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever he engages with, he engages through that third-person sense in a way that this is someone's else.

218
00:23:40,784 --> 00:23:41,384
[SPEAKER_00]: From the start.

219
00:23:42,465 --> 00:23:42,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Throughout.

220
00:23:43,930 --> 00:23:45,151
[SPEAKER_00]: throughout the duration of it.

221
00:23:45,791 --> 00:24:03,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, eh... if you frequently think and recollect that, that even things that are yours, including people, are not yours, cannot be yours, because at any given time they can be taken away, and... and it's unfathomable for you to be even able to, like, see how that will happen, or stop it, or... or be in the way of it.

222
00:24:03,745 --> 00:24:04,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

223
00:24:04,505 --> 00:24:06,867
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just gonna be subjected to it one way or the other.

224
00:24:07,928 --> 00:24:10,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about... See, none of these reflections are about

225
00:24:12,059 --> 00:24:17,104
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to think this through and arrive at some sort of resolution.

226
00:24:19,486 --> 00:24:26,913
[SPEAKER_00]: These reflections are not the management of your anxiety, of your fear of illness, of your fear of losing the loved ones.

227
00:24:27,513 --> 00:24:28,995
[SPEAKER_00]: They are actually supposed to bring it up.

228
00:24:29,985 --> 00:24:33,688
[SPEAKER_00]: And they're supposed to bring it up, because you're willingly choosing to ignore it.

229
00:24:34,288 --> 00:24:43,315
[SPEAKER_00]: And the benefits of bringing it up is to train your own mind to stop ignoring it, to stop resisting it, and to stop taking it for granted, because that's where the sense of ownership comes from.

230
00:24:43,896 --> 00:24:51,021
[SPEAKER_00]: You can tell, as the, again, like the your commonplace sort of Theravāda instructions, like, ah, you know, practice anatta, nothing is mine.

231
00:24:51,982 --> 00:24:55,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Electrons are constantly changing, collapses are constantly flowing and so on.

232
00:24:55,684 --> 00:25:01,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, you could be repeating that, parroting that for the rest of your life and no amount of your ownership would have diminished as a result of it.

233
00:25:03,069 --> 00:25:06,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the ownership diminishes when that wild animal ceases to be wild.

234
00:25:07,251 --> 00:25:09,052
[SPEAKER_00]: When that wild animal starts to listen to you.

235
00:25:09,072 --> 00:25:09,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Your own citta.

236
00:25:09,733 --> 00:25:13,015
[SPEAKER_00]: When your own citta cannot take possession of things.

237
00:25:14,727 --> 00:25:19,351
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for that, you need more than just these superficial mantras to repeat to yourself.

238
00:25:19,952 --> 00:25:21,313
[SPEAKER_00]: For that, you need these reflections.

239
00:25:21,353 --> 00:25:34,665
[SPEAKER_00]: For that, you need to feel the danger, the fear, the discomfort of the ideas of separation, illness, death.

240
00:25:35,294 --> 00:25:40,487
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, not just so if I feel this long enough, things will magically fall into place.

241
00:25:40,527 --> 00:25:40,868
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

242
00:25:41,429 --> 00:25:44,838
[SPEAKER_00]: I bring these feelings up because the mind doesn't want to look at it.

243
00:25:46,094 --> 00:26:04,218
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I bring it up, so that the mind learns how to... the animal learns to sit down and calm down in face of these things, and not pressure me and push me and goad me and prod me to go back to sensuality and provide its distraction, so it continues thinking away from the obvious nature of your experience.

244
00:26:04,618 --> 00:26:09,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Sickness, aging, death, separation from everything that's agreeable and dear to you.

245
00:26:10,519 --> 00:26:13,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not to, like, torture your mind either,

246
00:26:14,525 --> 00:26:17,366
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really the reference point for these reflections.

247
00:26:17,727 --> 00:26:23,389
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, the more you do these reflections, the clearer will be... your mind will be outlined as well.

248
00:26:23,409 --> 00:26:28,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where the resistance will be coming from, in regard to these things.

249
00:26:28,551 --> 00:26:33,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why frequently recollecting this can lead to the right view in and of itself.

250
00:26:34,874 --> 00:26:40,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, paired with virtue and the prior withdrawal from everything unwholesome you should have withdrawn, as the previous sutta said.

251
00:26:41,517 --> 00:26:42,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

252
00:26:43,919 --> 00:26:48,906
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to expect like a perfect contemplation or perfect results of this contemplation right from the start.

253
00:26:49,387 --> 00:26:59,981
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to kind of provoke the mind out of that sense of indolence, out of that sense of ignorance, negligence and non-self-awareness.

254
00:27:00,782 --> 00:27:08,286
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's probably going to be felt unpleasantly in the beginning, but again, you're not doing it for the sake of, like, amplifying that discomfort or something.

255
00:27:08,306 --> 00:27:14,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, sometimes people might feel, okay, so if I double down and increase this pain of this fear, somehow things will be quicker.

256
00:27:14,249 --> 00:27:16,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Not necessarily.

257
00:27:16,250 --> 00:27:17,371
[SPEAKER_00]: The measure is the mind.

258
00:27:17,711 --> 00:27:20,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you reflect these things, on these things,

259
00:27:21,620 --> 00:27:28,542
[SPEAKER_00]: You have maybe some feelings, some resistance come up, and then you leave it there, and try not not sort of get distracted from that.

260
00:27:29,102 --> 00:27:34,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as the other suttas say, you want to be furnishing your mind with these perceptions.

261
00:27:34,924 --> 00:27:39,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind is looking one way, and that's the way you are showing it these perceptions.

262
00:27:39,226 --> 00:27:41,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It looks like, oh, here's a bit of impermanence.

263
00:27:41,746 --> 00:27:42,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, you want to see this?

264
00:27:42,547 --> 00:27:46,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can look at it, but look at it through the contemplation of illness.

265
00:27:46,608 --> 00:27:49,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Look at it through the contemplation of losing the things that are dear to you.

266
00:27:50,544 --> 00:27:57,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how the mind will then realize that it cannot outrun... that... that... also it will realize that ignorance doesn't work.

267
00:27:59,308 --> 00:28:01,929
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why avijja feeds avijja, as the sutta says, basically.

268
00:28:02,069 --> 00:28:05,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Through ignoring, you genuinely convince yourself that the problem is gone.

269
00:28:06,532 --> 00:28:09,193
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's... it's... it's on the level of citta does that already.

270
00:28:10,159 --> 00:28:17,589
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by frequently recollecting on these things, it will eventually sink down into the mind.

271
00:28:17,649 --> 00:28:21,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Citta will understand that even when you ignore stuff, these things are there.

272
00:28:22,976 --> 00:28:27,081
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that belief that your ignorance works, will be diminished.

273
00:28:28,593 --> 00:28:32,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Eventually, it will be completely uprooted, if you stick to these reflections.

274
00:28:32,614 --> 00:28:42,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even monks were encouraged to contemplate death, contemplate these things, because it's like... like the quickest way to basically teach the mind what really needs to understand, and it's the quickest way towards freedom.

275
00:28:43,598 --> 00:28:56,922
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... that's kind of the point, that... through recollecting these things, not for the sake of psychologizing your way out of pain, or... or doubling down, like, through the ascetic, like, bearing of the pain, and just... just bringing more of this context.

276
00:28:56,942 --> 00:28:57,242
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

277
00:28:57,422 --> 00:28:58,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to, like,

278
00:29:01,062 --> 00:29:09,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Provide, let's say like that, provide a certain discomfort to your mind, and then leave it with it, until it gets used to it.

279
00:29:10,446 --> 00:29:16,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't need to be terribly overwhelming, or anything like that, but it needs to be there.

280
00:29:16,369 --> 00:29:20,291
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of resonance needs to be there, and it's not going to be felt like resonance.

281
00:29:20,311 --> 00:29:27,754
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be felt like dissonance, probably, because, as I said, all the desires and everything else is leaning towards looking away from it.

282
00:29:28,844 --> 00:29:36,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you've provided long enough, if you reflect on these things long enough, it's not that you will become incapable of forgetting things.

283
00:29:37,169 --> 00:29:40,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, forgetfulness is just the nature of the five aggregates.

284
00:29:41,011 --> 00:29:41,791
[SPEAKER_00]: It's how things work.

285
00:29:42,772 --> 00:29:52,057
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will be unable to maintain and carry further the belief that forgetfulness exempted you from these contexts.

286
00:29:53,110 --> 00:29:54,911
[SPEAKER_00]: That connection will be severed.

287
00:29:55,231 --> 00:29:57,891
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what means, like, avijjā is destroyed.

288
00:29:57,911 --> 00:30:00,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that arahant is constantly thinking about everything all the time.

289
00:30:01,773 --> 00:30:01,933
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

290
00:30:02,033 --> 00:30:07,814
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that even when he forgets, that forgetting happens within his recollectedness.

291
00:30:08,395 --> 00:30:16,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Recollectedness of certain contexts that no forgetfulness can induce any old beliefs of, oh, I'm actually

292
00:30:17,157 --> 00:30:22,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Separated from those implications, those things do not apply to me anymore.

293
00:30:23,004 --> 00:30:37,320
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the power of distraction, the power of sensuality, because for as long as that belief, that connection with the vijja exists, then giving in to those directions of distraction, even if you keep telling to yourself, oh, I know this is unbeneficial,

294
00:30:38,260 --> 00:30:48,285
[SPEAKER_00]: On the inside, your mind, your citta, still believes that it does actually, at least for that duration, free you from these pressures of these inevitable contexts.

295
00:30:49,966 --> 00:30:53,488
[SPEAKER_00]: But once that belief is severed, well, your work's pretty much done then.

296
00:30:54,588 --> 00:31:07,455
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be able to return to sensuality, because the sole purpose of the entire sensual domain was to maintain that momentum of that belief that makes you basically feel free from the problems of this fundamental existential nature.

297
00:31:10,272 --> 00:31:11,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the benefit of that.

298
00:31:12,013 --> 00:31:24,215
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, what's the benefit because of which a woman or a man, householder, one gone false, should reflect that I am the owner of my actions, heir to my actions, have actions as my origin, actions as my relative, actions as my resort.

299
00:31:24,495 --> 00:31:26,815
[SPEAKER_00]: I will be the heir to whatever actions, good or bad, that I do.

300
00:31:27,335 --> 00:31:32,096
[SPEAKER_00]: I already said, kind of talked about it in the... when we did the... just now, the previous... the first sutta.

301
00:31:33,476 --> 00:31:35,277
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically recognizing that

302
00:31:37,243 --> 00:31:40,726
[SPEAKER_00]: The wholesome or unwholesome or neutral actions are determined by your intentions.

303
00:31:42,388 --> 00:31:47,172
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that kind of brings the weight of the responsibility on the intentions.

304
00:31:47,192 --> 00:31:54,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Because usually, in the beginning, the weight of the responsibility of one's actions are pretty much on the results of one's actions.

305
00:31:54,878 --> 00:32:01,244
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, you know, I'll steal for it, and I'll lie, and I'll feel the weight of it only if I get caught.

306
00:32:01,904 --> 00:32:03,106
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how many people operate.

307
00:32:03,861 --> 00:32:08,125
[SPEAKER_00]: If I don't get caught, if I don't obviously harm anybody, it's not unwholesome.

308
00:32:08,165 --> 00:32:09,867
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see it as a problem and they'll do it.

309
00:32:11,028 --> 00:32:26,822
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start recognizing that the action begins with your intention, means, oh, you're already incurring a lot of unwholesome, including, like, the actual results of those actions are already felt, even if you don't necessarily see them.

310
00:32:27,102 --> 00:32:29,645
[SPEAKER_00]: And those results are felt on the level of that wild animal.

311
00:32:30,813 --> 00:32:41,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I often say, as I said in those previous talks as well, everything might feel fine, even for people that don't keep the precepts, you know.

312
00:32:41,596 --> 00:32:45,156
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that they're like unhappy or something because of it.

313
00:32:45,897 --> 00:32:49,637
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that they're going to pay a higher price and they're already paying it without realizing that.

314
00:32:50,378 --> 00:32:59,700
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like when the wild animal is allowed the wilderness, it's allowed to do what he wants, it's not being tamed.

315
00:33:00,500 --> 00:33:09,127
[SPEAKER_00]: You're basically increasing its capacity for wilderness, its capacity for being irrational and crazy and wild and prone to passion.

316
00:33:10,067 --> 00:33:17,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, when that animal turns around and bites you, which it will, when illness happens, when you lose... inevitably has to happen, it will bite you a lot harder.

317
00:33:19,274 --> 00:33:24,958
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the state of the mind, of an untrained mind, it's not a neutral, it's not like a static thing.

318
00:33:25,018 --> 00:33:28,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, this is untrained and it's... no, it grows and it knows no limits.

319
00:33:30,126 --> 00:33:39,114
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the time comes, when that mind doesn't get what it wants, it will turn against you and it will bite you a lot harder, a lot more viciously.

320
00:33:40,147 --> 00:33:42,268
[SPEAKER_00]: It will cause a lot more damage.

321
00:33:42,288 --> 00:33:47,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the mind, let's say, was significantly tamed and still turns around and starts blaming you for something.

322
00:33:48,051 --> 00:33:49,392
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the difference.

323
00:33:49,432 --> 00:34:00,738
[SPEAKER_00]: I keep telling to people, even when, you know, when they get... when they're caught up in these mental patterns of self-loathing and blame and it's just with all this force and power for years and they can't get rid of it.

324
00:34:01,699 --> 00:34:02,839
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the reason why.

325
00:34:05,180 --> 00:34:07,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your mind has no respect for you.

326
00:34:08,811 --> 00:34:15,393
[SPEAKER_00]: If you take that away, no amount of your traumas and stuff from the past, and the mind trying to revolve around it, can hit you as hard.

327
00:34:30,796 --> 00:34:33,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're taking away the sail from its wind.

328
00:34:33,878 --> 00:34:34,698
[SPEAKER_00]: The sail from its wind.

329
00:34:35,779 --> 00:34:36,759
[SPEAKER_00]: The wind from its sail.

330
00:34:37,980 --> 00:34:39,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point.

331
00:34:39,340 --> 00:34:42,642
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a static thing, just like black or white.

332
00:34:42,682 --> 00:34:44,063
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like your actions matter.

333
00:34:44,883 --> 00:34:49,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you feeding the wilderness, the craziness of the animal, or are you reducing it?

334
00:34:50,718 --> 00:35:02,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So, although it still hasn't turned around to bite you, the fact is that you're cultivating the wilderness of the animal, means you're already basically deciding what type of bite it will be when it happens.

335
00:35:02,265 --> 00:35:06,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, the results of your actions are already being developed there.

336
00:35:08,028 --> 00:35:11,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Even without anything obviously palpable and so on, at the time.

337
00:35:12,318 --> 00:35:15,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's important to always, like, I'm the owner of my actions.

338
00:35:16,581 --> 00:35:19,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I... they cannot go on to anybody else.

339
00:35:20,383 --> 00:35:23,045
[SPEAKER_00]: They will remain bound with me and this mind.

340
00:35:23,165 --> 00:35:25,266
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no third option there, basically.

341
00:35:25,306 --> 00:35:26,647
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no second option in that way.

342
00:35:27,528 --> 00:35:29,809
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why these reflections are on the same level.

343
00:35:30,250 --> 00:35:35,533
[SPEAKER_00]: The reflection about action and the ownership and the roots of it, which is your intentions.

344
00:35:35,753 --> 00:35:40,216
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they're on the level of contemplating sickness, aging, death, separation and all of it.

345
00:35:40,256 --> 00:35:41,397
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that fundamental.

346
00:35:44,502 --> 00:35:50,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, says the the noble disciple, reflects thus, I'm not the only one who is subject to old age.

347
00:35:50,227 --> 00:36:06,843
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, see, you reflect like this, the way we described it, it will eventually, as I said, outline your mind more and more simply through that sheer gratuitous resistance of the mind in regard to these contemplations.

348
00:36:08,835 --> 00:36:11,825
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, eh... that's... that's pretty much what sotāpatti will be.

349
00:36:12,126 --> 00:36:15,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when you see your mind that animal clearly, when the...

350
00:36:16,223 --> 00:36:22,146
[SPEAKER_00]: A certain layer of identification with it is abolished through that seeing it.

351
00:36:22,606 --> 00:36:30,551
[SPEAKER_00]: See, through perpetually not seeing the animal, through habitually not seeing the extent, the outlines, the nimittas of your mind, that's the ātavāda.

352
00:36:30,571 --> 00:36:31,771
[SPEAKER_00]: The result of that is ātavāda.

353
00:36:31,831 --> 00:36:36,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Ātavāda is not your theoretical conviction that I am self and so on.

354
00:36:36,714 --> 00:36:36,854
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

355
00:36:36,954 --> 00:36:37,654
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the symptom.

356
00:36:37,714 --> 00:36:38,315
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the result

357
00:36:39,195 --> 00:36:46,839
[SPEAKER_00]: of an underlying ignorance, looking past and non-recognition of what the citta is.

358
00:36:48,139 --> 00:37:05,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you would have been outlining it through precepts, restraint and these recollections frequently dwelled upon, the citta will be seen and then, whether you want or not, you will be unable to identify with it and assume yourself as some sort of external owner to the whole thing.

359
00:37:05,587 --> 00:37:06,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So, attavāda will be gone.

360
00:37:07,692 --> 00:37:22,677
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the noble disciple, once he's kind of cultivated and established in these recollections, will then also know that I'm not... and will be able to remind himself as well, I'm not the only one who is subject to old age, exempt from old age.

361
00:37:23,597 --> 00:37:28,521
[SPEAKER_00]: All beings that come and go, that arise and pass away, they undergo the same, basically, nature.

362
00:37:29,001 --> 00:37:34,425
[SPEAKER_00]: They're subject to old age, rebirth and birth old age and not exempt from old age.

363
00:37:35,346 --> 00:37:37,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Subject to old age and non-exempt from old age.

364
00:37:37,107 --> 00:37:37,368
[SPEAKER_00]: I see.

365
00:37:38,208 --> 00:37:40,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he will often reflect on this theme.

366
00:37:41,270 --> 00:37:44,293
[SPEAKER_00]: The path is generated even clearer.

367
00:37:44,493 --> 00:37:48,856
[SPEAKER_00]: He pursues it, develops it and cultivates it and then all the facts will be abandoned.

368
00:37:49,336 --> 00:37:50,878
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is like a condensed way

369
00:37:52,121 --> 00:37:56,503
[SPEAKER_00]: See, like, you would have contemplated enough that you're subject to old age.

370
00:37:57,064 --> 00:37:58,764
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind would have been outlined and so on.

371
00:37:59,605 --> 00:38:05,408
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, within that, say you would have abandoned ātavāda, you are a sotāpanna, but within that, there is still that sense of I am.

372
00:38:06,546 --> 00:38:12,850
[SPEAKER_00]: That still lingers through some perversion of perception in regard to the ownership, in regard to permanence.

373
00:38:12,930 --> 00:38:25,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not on the scale of a pūtugena, it's not on the extent of a pūtugena's mind, who doesn't see any of it, so he's like really subjected to the whole thing, but there is still remnants, the residues.

374
00:38:25,959 --> 00:38:35,526
[SPEAKER_00]: As the suttas say, it's like, you know, pūtugena would have been stuck with a poison arrow, that's still like emitting poison into the wound,

375
00:38:36,792 --> 00:38:39,895
[SPEAKER_00]: But a noble disciple would have had arrow pulled out.

376
00:38:40,415 --> 00:38:42,957
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is still now the residual poison left.

377
00:38:43,838 --> 00:38:49,662
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, he needs to follow a certain diet, anoint the wound, clean it, until that's gone as well.

378
00:38:50,223 --> 00:38:51,864
[SPEAKER_00]: There's secondary infections and so on.

379
00:38:52,224 --> 00:38:54,366
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what these recollections say.

380
00:38:54,426 --> 00:38:58,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the path will be generated in a sotāpanna, the path that he knows,

381
00:39:00,316 --> 00:39:03,698
[SPEAKER_00]: will be highlighted if he keeps reminding himself of this.

382
00:39:04,399 --> 00:39:27,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is... like, again, even... even to the extent that I understood and to the extent that I do have a conceit of I am separate from this person or that person, well, to that extent, you kind of recognize, well, but this I am, that might feel separate to that subtler extent, not like a putugenous level of conceit, but even that cannot be exempted from it.

383
00:39:28,382 --> 00:39:30,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that cannot not partake in the same nature.

384
00:39:31,384 --> 00:39:35,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, it's just practicing what Adi Sutta says a noble disciple should practice.

385
00:39:35,907 --> 00:39:37,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Not mine, not for me, not I am.

386
00:39:38,268 --> 00:39:51,337
[SPEAKER_00]: And he reminds himself through these four or five aspects of sickness, aging, death, losing things that you're connected with and that you are the owner of your actions.

387
00:39:52,457 --> 00:39:53,337
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm not the only one.

388
00:39:53,818 --> 00:39:55,158
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not the only one subject to death.

389
00:39:55,759 --> 00:39:58,720
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not the only one who must be parted and separated from everybody.

390
00:39:59,260 --> 00:40:01,061
[SPEAKER_00]: All beings that come and go.

391
00:40:01,101 --> 00:40:03,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no exception, basically.

392
00:40:03,202 --> 00:40:05,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Not even, not even, like, in terms of the human realm.

393
00:40:05,523 --> 00:40:10,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Just in any realm, in any shape or form, this is, like, inescapable.

394
00:40:10,866 --> 00:40:17,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means any lingering conceit of I am, that to that extent, I might be separate a little bit for a period of time, you're not.

395
00:40:19,041 --> 00:40:20,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that's included in it.

396
00:40:20,842 --> 00:40:23,703
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what a noble disciple wants to be practicing.

397
00:40:23,923 --> 00:40:29,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Including the sense of conceit, the little desires that he still has left, and so on.

398
00:40:29,926 --> 00:40:35,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Re-including them into this context until it fully permeates all of the layers that are there that need to be permeated.

399
00:40:37,049 --> 00:40:42,491
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why then, all the fetters will eventually be abandoned and the underlying tendency completely uprooted.

400
00:40:42,891 --> 00:40:44,512
[SPEAKER_00]: So, arahantship and complete freedom.

401
00:40:45,737 --> 00:40:56,187
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sutta finishes with these verses saying that warlings, putujanas, are subject to illness, old age, death, and are disgusted by other people who exist in accordance with their nature.

402
00:40:57,192 --> 00:41:10,820
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they feel, like, quite... to an irrational degree, in a sense, to, like, a degree of madness, they feel separated from the obvious things that they see others go through, and they feel disgusted with them.

403
00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:14,322
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how far gone the putujanas are.

404
00:41:14,442 --> 00:41:23,547
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how... how exempt from those things they feel, that actually feel justified in their disgust of that which is, like, they're partaking in.

405
00:41:24,918 --> 00:41:35,506
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, a noble disciple wouldn't be going that far, but then he should remind himself even further that even the slightest disgust with what I perceive in others, I must re-include myself in it.

406
00:41:36,206 --> 00:41:38,368
[SPEAKER_00]: So, until there is no conceit left, basically.

407
00:41:39,288 --> 00:41:43,672
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I were to become disgusted with beings who have such a nature, that would not be proper.

408
00:41:44,032 --> 00:41:47,895
[SPEAKER_00]: That would not be aligned with the path, basically, since I too have the same nature.

409
00:41:48,951 --> 00:41:56,641
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha says, while I was dwelling this, having known the state without acquisitions, without ownership, he overcome all intoxications.

410
00:41:57,442 --> 00:42:06,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Intoxication with health, youth, life, and having seen security in renunciation, means security in giving up sensual desires.

411
00:42:08,630 --> 00:42:14,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Sīla rose in me and I clearly saw nibbāna and recognized that he's incapable of indulging in sensual pleasure.

412
00:42:14,573 --> 00:42:16,453
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I said before.

413
00:42:17,014 --> 00:42:18,594
[SPEAKER_00]: You frequently recollect these things.

414
00:42:20,115 --> 00:42:29,599
[SPEAKER_00]: All those cover-ups that serve as maintenance of the belief in your avijjā will become redundant, because you would have destroyed the belief in your avijjā.

415
00:42:29,819 --> 00:42:32,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether I ignore this or not, the context remains the same.

416
00:42:33,081 --> 00:42:35,742
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of what I'm subjected to remains the same.

417
00:42:37,753 --> 00:42:40,616
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the whole purpose of sensuality is removed.

418
00:42:41,276 --> 00:42:47,882
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it says, like, once you clearly understood that, he recognized that he's incapable of indulging in sensual pleasures.

419
00:42:48,042 --> 00:42:50,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, the reason for them is gone.

420
00:42:50,785 --> 00:42:53,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Relying on this spiritual life, never will I turn back.

421
00:42:59,252 --> 00:43:04,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that concludes, pretty much, these two suttas.

